From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:45:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] platform: arm64: add HP OmniBook (Snapdragon X2) EC driver

The EC of the HP OmniBook Ultra 14 (Snapdragon X2) sits on I2C at 0x76
and takes commands through the mailbox HP's ACPI method HQEX drives
(idle status 0xef, command 0xed, 32-byte reply 0xee); its interrupt
signals events, read from register 0x00 and acknowledged by writing 0.
Protocol and most codes match the HP OmniBook 5 16 EC, so the driver
is based on Rahul Pon's driver for that laptop.

Expose the fan speed and the thermistors of HP's three enabled EC
thermal zones through hwmon; the keyboard backlight level (which HP's
ACPI has no command to set) and the mute and mic mute LED states as
LEDs without a set callback; the keyboard backlight timeout as a sysfs
attribute, the only EC state the driver writes; the function-key lock
as a read-only attribute; and the F9 (mic mute) and F11 hotkeys, which
only the EC reports, as input events.

The event line is edge-triggered, so every read drains up to 8 events,
and the handler also runs at probe and resume for edges it missed. The
interrupt is not a wakeup source, as fan and temperature events must
not wake the laptop. The "S4 hot" events, on which HP's ACPI asks the
OS to hibernate, are only logged.

Tested on the OmniBook Ultra 14-kg0xxx; the hotkey and event handling
has not been exercised with key presses.

Link: https://github.com/theflyingrahul/linux-hp-omnibook-5-glymur
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
